  "description": {
    "en": "The Spanish federation's dan grade programme, from 1st to 5th Dan, as approved in October 2025 and in force from 1 January 2026. Each grade sets its own list of standing and groundwork techniques, its fundamentals, a tokui waza the candidate develops at length, and one kata.",
    "es": "El programa de grados dan de la RFEJYDA, de 1º a 5º Dan, aprobado en octubre de 2025 y en vigor desde el 1 de enero de 2026."
  },
  "source": {
    "title": "Contenidos de los programas de 1º a 6º Dan de Judo",
    "date": "October 2025",
    "note": {
      "en": "Transcribed from the RFEJYDA programme approved by the Junta Directiva in October 2025 and in force from 1 January 2026. Item text is the Spanish as printed, including the source's own typographical slips (\"trs contras\", \"do salidas\", and \"Kuzure-Tsukuri-Kake\" where the standard term is Kuzushi). Four things a reader should know. Some techniques are printed in red, which the programme defines as explained but not executed: Hane makikomi and Uchi mata makikomi at 3rd Dan, O soto maki komi and Kawazu gake at 4th Dan, and Kani basami at 5th Dan. Colour cannot be carried in the data, so it is recorded here. The 5th Dan page announces fifteen standing techniques and prints thirteen; the announcement is transcribed as printed and the list is what the document actually names. Several techniques this programme examines are documented in this reference for recognition only, because they are prohibited outright: Kawazu gake, Kani basami, Do jime and Ashi garami. And 6th Dan carries an EMPTY techniqueSlugs, which is the honest record rather than a gap: it prescribes no techniques at all, being free-choice tachi waza and ne waza work submitted to the territorial school thirty days in advance, plus Kodokan Goshin-jutsu. The programme prints the same paragraph for both, so the ne waza block also says \"trabajo de libre elección de judo pie\" - standing judo - which is transcribed as printed like the rest of the document's slips."
    }
  },
